.\" Manpage for clone.
.TH man 1 "30 September 2024" "1.0" "clone man page"
.SH NAME
clone.sh \- clone one drive to another using \fBrsync\fR(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clone.sh\fR \fI[\fB-e\fI exclude_file] [\fB-f\fI fstypes_file] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Source and destination drives need not be the same size as long as all source data fits on destination. If the source drive is bootable the destination will become bootable as well. 

If you run \fIclone.sh\fR without any command line options the configuration files used are the defaults under \fI/etc/clone/\fR which can optionally be overriden under \fI~/.config/clone/\fR.

After you run \fIclone.sh\fR a list of files/dirs excluded from cloning is displayed, e.g. system files under \fI/dev/*\fR, \fI/proc/*\fR, \fI/sys/*\fR, etc. These files/dirs are read from the configuration file \fIexclude\fR (see \fBFILES\fR). Also, a list of drives connected to your system is displayed and you will be asked to input source and destination drives (by number). 

During cloning, appropriate messages are produced on standard output indicating which command is being executed and a progress indicator, including total bytes and % cloned, is displayed as well. Log files are produced under \fI/var/log/clone.sh_cdb68a8f-2050-4fcd-a789-c130cc98c1e5/\fR.

You can run more than one \fIclone.sh\fR instance at a time provided the destinations are (obviously) different. So, if three drives are connected to your system one drive can be cloned to the other two in parallel.
.SH EXAMPLES
After you run \fIclone.sh\fR you will be prompted with a user-friendly menu where the drives connected to your system are displayed. Just type in the source and destination by number separated by a space, e.g. 1 2.
.SH FILES
The configuration files used by \fIclone.sh\fR are:

\fI/etc/clone/exclude\fR (see \fBman \fIclone-exclude\fR)

\fI/etc/clone/fstypes\fR (contains commands to format a newly created partition for a given filesystem)

\fI/etc/clone/grub_modules\fR (contains GRUB 2 modules required to install the GRUB 2 bootloader on destination)

The user can create override files under \fI~/.config/clone/\fR but the default filenames have to be used. It is recommended not to modify the default \fIfstypes\fR and \fIgrub_modules\fR files or create your own unless you really know what you are doing.

\fBclone.sh\fR searches for override files if not invoked under \fI/usr/local/bin/clone/\fR. The location of the override files must be under \fI~/.config/clone/\fR and the default filenames have to be used.

Override files can also be provided when invoking \fBclone.sh\fR with the \fB-e\fR or \fB-f\fR command line options (see \fIOPTIONS\fR). This takes precedence over any other files and any pathnames can be used.

Every time a cloning session is executed existing log directories are deleted and the directory \fI/var/log/clone.sh_cdb68a8f-2050-4fcd-a789-c130cc98c1e5/x_y/\fR is created where 'x' is the number of the source drive and 'y' is the number of the destination drive. The following files are created under this directory:

\fIlog\fR      (contains files/dirs cloned)

\fIerrors\fR   (contains any errors encountered)

\fIcommands\fR (contains most commands executed by \fIclone.sh\fR)

Sometimes it is useful to \fBtail\fR(1) the \fIlog\fR file to see which files/dirs are being transferred by \fBrsync\fR(1).

Before cloning the following directory and lock files under it are created:

\fI/var/lock/clone.sh_cdb68a8f-2050-4fcd-a789-c130cc98c1e5/\fR

    \fIclone_pids\fR: ensure that \fIclone.sh\fR is executed only if its source and destination are not destinations of another \fIclone.sh\fR instance

    \fIhbn_clone_pid\fR: ensure that only one \fIclone.sh\fR instance is responsible for masking/unmasking hibernation

The lock directory and its contents are deleted before \fBclone.sh\fR exits.

In case multiple clone sessions are being executed in parallel there will be a log directory per session.
.SH OPTIONS
\fB-e\fR \fIexclude_file\fR

\fB-f\fR \fIfstypes_file\fR

If any are provided they override the default configuration files (see \fBFILES\fR). Must be valid pathnames.
.SH LIMITS
The source drive must have partitions with UUIDs.

The source drive must not use Logical Volume Management (LVM).

If the source drive has partitions with the btrfs filesystem any subvolumes on them will not be created on the destination. To see which filesystems are fully supported check \fIfstypes\fR file (see \fBFILES\fR).

\fIclone.sh\fR must be invoked on a system that uses systemd as init.

If the source drive is bootable the destination will become bootable only if the source drive and the system \fIclone.sh\fR is invoked on use GRUB 2 as their bootloader. Regardless, the destination will contain all source data provided cloning was successful.

.SH EXIT_STATUS
Zero on success else > 1. In case of error an appropriate error message is displayed and further info on the error might be available in the error file (see \fBFILES\fR).
.SH NOTES
To clone, \fIclone.sh\fR is using the \fBrsync\fR(1) \fI--inc-recursive\fR (\fI--i-r\fR) command line option in order to use much less memory. However, this option interferes with the \fBrsync\fR(1) \fI--progress\fR command line option. The percentage of files transferred that is displayed is incorrect although the data size transferred is correct.
.SH CAVEATS
In case the user has many programs running while cloning then it is possible that some files that these programs use are deleted during cloning. However, the deleted files are part of \fBrsync\fR(1)'s list of files to transfer. In that case, when \fBrsync\fR(1) exits, its return code is 24 (partial transfer due to vanished source files) but \fIclone.sh\fR ignores it. Therefore, it is recommended that prior to invoking \fIclone.sh\fR the user closes all programs on all user accounts and exits all user accounts but the one that will be used to invoke \fIclone.sh\fR.
.SH BUGS
If \fIclone.sh\fR is invoked from a XFCE terminal window and hibernation is masked by \fIclone.sh\fR and the hibernation period expires while \fBrsync\fR(1) is running then when cloning has completed and hibernation is unmasked by \fIclone.sh\fR, XFCE can not hibernate. However, if the terminal window is closed, XFCE can hibernate after the hibernation period has expired again.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBclone-exclude\fR(5), \fBrsync\fR(1), \fBparted\fR(8), \fBgdisk\fR(8), \fBmkfs\fR(8), \fBsystemd\fR(1), \fBgrub-install\fR(8), \fBgrub-mkconfig\fR(8)
